## 1. Executive Summary

This matrix synthesizes all tested numerical, algorithmic, and formulation factors investigated to explain why literal execution of the published Python workflow (Listing 1: `errorTarget=1.0`, $h_{\min}=0.001\,\text{mm}, h_{\max}=0.020\,\text{mm}, \text{refinementFactor}=10, \text{coarseningFactor}=\text{NOT\_ALLOWED}$) produces **71,320 finite elements** across Abaqus 2021, 2022, and 2023 on Linux, while Section 4.1 (p. 3265, Line 1027) reports approximately **13,941 finite elements**.

Every factor is assigned an explicit epistemic classification:
- `RULED_OUT`: Proven by rigorous numerical/topological testing to have zero effect or to be impossible under the native Abaqus workflow.
- `TESTED_NO_MATERIAL_EFFECT`: Evaluated across realistic parameter ranges with no substantial change toward 13,941.
- `PLAUSIBLE_UNTESTED`: Potentially influential mechanism that has not yet been isolated by a controlled one-factor test.
- `PUBLICATION_INFORMATION_MISSING` / `PUBLICATION_LINKAGE_AMBIGUOUS`: Verified parameter, rule, or setting omitted from the publication text/listings.
- `RELEASE_PLUS_PLATFORM_CONFOUNDED` / `CONFOUNDED`: Tested configuration that simultaneously varied multiple numerical/formulation parameters or produced secondary artifacts.

